WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.280
ED GLAESER: The tallest building in the world is surrounded by desert and sea.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:09.140
The Burj Khalifa source in a city that had fewer than 50,000 people in 1960.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.030
Less than half the population of Sommerville, Massachusetts then.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:17.230
In 50 years, Dubai's population has grown nearly 50 fold.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:20.940
And now that city has practically become synonymous with glittering oil wealth and

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.360
high tech urbanism.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.110
Through the 18th century Dubai was essentially a remote fishing village

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:30.908
anchored by a creek that still divides the city into two halves, Deira and Bur Dubai.

00:00:30.908 --> 00:00:35.430
In the 19th century Dubai broke away from the Sheikhdom of Abu Dhabi, but

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.800
both became Trucial States.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.860
Emirates that were allied with Great Britain and

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:43.170
ultimately part of a British protectorate they would last until 1971.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:48.098
The Port of Dubai achieved modest success during the British era as a trading

00:00:48.098 --> 00:00:48.877
entrepot.

00:00:48.877 --> 00:00:52.850
And occasional stop over for steam ships traveling to and from India.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.556
Dubai Sheikhs were always commerce friendly and

00:00:55.556 --> 00:00:58.990
they did their best to attract Indian and Iranian merchants.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:02.677
Dubai's most lustrous products were pearls, hunted along the coast for

00:01:02.677 --> 00:01:03.830
centuries.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.680
And their prices suffered with the introduction of cultured pearls in

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.740
the middle of the 20th century.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:14.280
The seeds for Dubai's post-pearl success were largely laid by Sheikh Rashid II,

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.840
the Emir from 1958 to 1990 during whose reign the city's population grew

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.210
from 40,000 to over 400,000.

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:24.710
The Sheik envisioned Dubai as being the urban connector between his region

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:25.900
and the world.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.780
And he tried to achieve that end by investing heavily in infrastructure.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.960
He dredged Dubai's creek and then built a bridge across it.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.920
He built Dubai's first airport and

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.610
despite British skepticism enlarged it to allow jet airplanes in 1965.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:43.100
He opened Port Rashid, named for himself, of course, in 1972.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.110
And then Jebel Ali port seven years later.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:50.310
Jebel Ali is supposedly the largest man made harbor in the world.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:55.055
The Jebel Ali port was joined by the Jebel Ali Free Zone six years later.

00:01:55.055 --> 00:01:56.925
Free zones provide lightly taxed and

00:01:56.925 --> 00:02:00.915
lightly regulated regions to attract international firms and industries.

00:02:00.915 --> 00:02:03.965
In a sense, they provide business friendly legal infrastructure

00:02:03.965 --> 00:02:07.345
to match the region's business friendly physical infrastructure.

00:02:07.345 --> 00:02:11.605
Betting big on physical infrastructure can easily go awry, and

00:02:11.605 --> 00:02:15.510
Sheikh Rashid's early investments surely seemed like big gambles.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:19.530
Today's investments also have real downside risk, but

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.860
the Sheikh's gambles paid off well.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.230
As he put it, my grandfather rode a camel.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:25.720
My father rode a camel.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.290
I drive a Mercedes.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.090
And, his success owed a lot to oil.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.630
The oil wealth in neighboring Abu Dhabi had been discovered in the 1950s,

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.350
just before Sheikh Rashid began his reign.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:40.370
Dubai yet didn't have it's own oil but the Sheikh grasped how Dubai could get

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:46.090
rich later, shipping the oil of others, serving as the region's conduit to the outside world.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:48.860
Dubai's own oil was discovered in 1965 but

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.200
much of that wealth was ploughed back in to building the city.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.790
Sheikh Rashid was convinced that without investing in the city

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:58.150
his grandson would end up riding a camel like his fore bears.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:01.090
While some of the chieftans prefer to remain more conservative and

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:04.630
closed, Dubai has been relatively open to the outside world and

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:07.348
keenly aware of its need to attract outside talent.

00:03:07.348 --> 00:03:11.080
More than 80% of the city's inhabitants are immigrants.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.830
Above all, from India, and they are there, not just because of the oil,

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.870
the failings of India's legal and physical infrastructure.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.420
And the easy air travel to well-managed Dubai

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.110
makes Dubai an attractive place to do business.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:26.630
You can certainly meet professionals in Mumbai, who spend their weeks working in

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:30.000
the Emirates and come home to their families on the weekend.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.380
Dubai's recent building burst has captured the imagination of the world.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:37.300
The worlds tallest skyscraper, the Burj Khalifa, the world's third tallest hotel,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.390
the Burj Al Arab,

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:42.340
which sits on a man-made island, the first of three that are meant to be built.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.658
And the world's second largest shopping mall.

00:03:44.658 --> 00:03:48.140
In a sense, Dubai's great building boom can be seen as an attempt

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:51.760
to keep winning in the global contest to attract human capital.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.100
Dubai first made itself a good place to work.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.290
Now its shopping malls and skyscrapers and

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:01.060
its dolphin area can be seen as an attempt to create a consumer city as well.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.260
A place that is fun as well as productive.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.530
When talent is global,

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:09.240
investing in quality of life is an economic development strategy.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.350
One mantra is that cities should attract and train smart people and

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:14.300
then get out of their way.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:15.730
But has Dubai invested too much?

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.130
Certainly there were rough years in the Great Recession.

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:22.850
Dubai had to turn to its oil rich friends in Abu Dhabi for support.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:26.130
The Emirate is again running a deficit in 2017.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:29.070
Aggressive infrastructure investment always runs the risk

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.800
of building too much too soon.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:33.650
Things seem to have stabilized.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:37.450
But I'd caution against any city taking the lesson in Dubai to be that cities

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:42.110
must have the snazziest, most over the top, most extreme physical infrastructure.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:46.040
The right lesson, the deeper lesson is that Dubai has long recognized that it

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:51.290
must attract outside talent to succeed and that is true for all cities.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:53.370
Human capital educated at home, and

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:57.770
imported from abroad is the real recipe for long run urban success.

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:00.900
An infrastructure only makes sense when it empowers and

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.840
brings value to the humanity that is the real city.

